//! The Bucket_Array module: a growable sequence whose element **addresses never move**.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.3 put this second in W7's remaining order because it needs nothing new — it
//! is `[..]T` (ADR-0136) used as a spine — and because it is what a UI retains handles into, which W10
//! will want.
//!
//! # Why this exists when `List` already does
//!
//! `List($T)` doubles its storage and **copies** on growth, so every pointer into it is invalidated by the
//! next `push`. That is the right trade for a sequence you iterate and the wrong one for a sequence you
//! hold references into — and holding a reference is not an exotic case: an entity that points at its
//! parent, a UI element that remembers its child, an interner that hands out a pointer to a stored value.
//!
//! A bucket array keeps a `[..]` of fixed-size **buckets** and only ever appends a bucket. Existing buckets
//! never move, so an element's address is stable for as long as the array lives.
//!
//! ```text
//!   spine:  [ *bucket0, *bucket1, *bucket2 ]     <- this may move on growth
//!   buckets: [16 slots] [16 slots] [16 slots]    <- these never move
//! ```
//!
//! The spine moves, and that is fine: nothing outside this module holds a pointer to the spine.
//!
//! # Why the bucket size is a constant rather than a parameter
//!
//! One size, chosen once, because the only reason to vary it is a measurement this project cannot take
//! (ADR-0146 §4: it measures its own throughput and deliberately not the programs it compiles). A
//! parameter would be a knob with no evidence behind it, and ADR-0104 §3 already refused a "faster"
//! algorithm chosen without a benchmark.
//!
//! Sixteen, because a bucket is one allocation and sixteen `s64`s is 128 bytes — small enough that a
//! two-element array wastes little, large enough that a thousand elements is 63 allocations rather than 1000.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No removal.** A bucket array's promise is that an address stays valid, and removal has to answer what
//! happens to the hole: compacting moves elements and breaks the promise, while leaving a tombstone means
//! every read has to check one and `get` stops being a pointer arithmetic. Both are real designs; neither
//! is decidable without knowing what the caller wants, so this offers append and read, which is what the
//! stability promise is *for*.
//!
//! **Concrete `s64`, not `$T`.** An imported *template* is still refused (E0268, ADR-0104 §5), so a
//! generic bucket array cannot cross a module boundary — the same limit `List` and `Map` were written
//! under before ADR-0118 made them generic *within* their module. When that refusal lifts this becomes
//! `Bucket_Array($T)` with no change to its shape.
//!
//! # Why the spine's operations are written here rather than imported from `List`
//!
//! `List`'s `push` operates on `[..]s64` and the spine is a `[..]*s64`, and an imported template cannot be
//! instantiated at a second element type (E0268) — so importing `List` would not help. Writing the two
//! operations the spine needs (append, free) locally is about thirty lines and is *concrete*, which is
//! exactly the shape ADR-0118 used inside `List` itself before genericity was available across modules.
//!
//! A `[..]T` also cannot be **indexed** directly — `only a fixed-size array [N]T and a view []T can be
//! indexed` — so the spine is read through `view(data, count)`, which is the same route `List.elements`
//! takes and the reason `view` exists (ADR-0109 §1).

#import "Basic";

/// How many elements one bucket holds. See the module docs for why this is a constant.
BUCKET_SIZE :: 16;

/// One bucket: a pointer to its sixteen slots.
///
/// A **named struct** wrapping the pointer rather than a bare `*s64`, because `size_of` and `typed` refuse
/// a *structural* type argument — `size_of(*s64)` is E0261, ADR-0071 §5's still-deferred structural type
/// argument. A named type is spellable, so `size_of(Bucket)` and `typed(Bucket, p)` work. It reads better
/// too: the spine is a list of buckets, not a list of pointers-to-integers.
Bucket :: struct {
    /// The bucket's sixteen slots.
    slots: *s64;
}

/// A growable sequence of `s64` whose element addresses never move.
///
/// `count` is the logical length; the last bucket is partly filled. Storage is `count` rounded up to a
/// bucket, which is the only overhead a caller pays for stability.
Bucket_Array :: struct {
    /// One pointer per bucket. **This** may move when it grows; the buckets it points at may not.
    spine: [..]Bucket;
    /// How many elements have been pushed.
    count: s64;
}

/// An empty bucket array, allocating nothing.
///
/// Nothing is allocated until the first `push`, so an array that is declared and never used is free — the
/// same property `List` has and for the same reason: a struct field of this type must not force an
/// allocation on its owner's construction.
make :: () -> Bucket_Array {
    b: Bucket_Array;
    b.count = 0;
    return b;
}

/// Appends `value` and returns a **stable pointer** to where it now lives.
///
/// The returned pointer stays valid for the life of the array — that is the whole reason this module
/// exists. It is returned rather than left to a following `get` call because the caller almost always wants
/// it immediately, and returning it makes the stability promise visible in the signature.
///
/// `null` if a bucket cannot be allocated, which is the one failure a caller might reasonably handle by
/// giving up rather than trapping.
push :: (b: *Bucket_Array, value: s64) -> *s64 {
    slot := b.count % BUCKET_SIZE;
    if slot == 0 {
        // A new bucket. `malloc` rather than a growable region, because a bucket's whole purpose is that
        // its address never changes, and only a fresh allocation can promise that.
        fresh := typed(s64, malloc(BUCKET_SIZE * size_of(s64)));
        if fresh == null {
            return null;
        }
        entry: Bucket;
        entry.slots = fresh;
        if !push_bucket(*b.spine, entry) {
            // The bucket was allocated and the spine could not record it, so it would leak. Released
            // here rather than left, because a failure path that leaks is a failure path nobody tests.
            free(untyped(fresh));
            return null;
        }
    }
    spine := view(b.spine.data, b.spine.count);
    bucket := spine[b.count / BUCKET_SIZE].slots;
    target := bucket + slot;
    target.* = value;
    b.count = b.count + 1;
    return target;
}

/// A pointer to element `index`, or `null` when it is out of range.
///
/// `null` rather than a trap, because an out-of-range read here is a *lookup that failed* rather than a
/// memory error: the caller has an index from somewhere and wants to know whether it names an element. A
/// trap is what indexing a `[N]T` does, and that is the right behaviour for a construct with a known length
/// (ADR-0003) — this one's length is data.
get :: (b: *Bucket_Array, index: s64) -> *s64 {
    if index < 0 {
        return null;
    }
    if index >= b.count {
        return null;
    }
    spine := view(b.spine.data, b.spine.count);
    bucket := spine[index / BUCKET_SIZE].slots;
    return bucket + index % BUCKET_SIZE;
}

/// Element `index`, or 0 when it is out of range.
///
/// The value form, for a caller that does not want the pointer. `0` for a missing element is a real answer
/// rather than a sentinel only because this array holds `s64` — when it becomes generic, this returns
/// whatever the element type's zero is, which is the same thing a default-initialised local gets.
value_at :: (b: *Bucket_Array, index: s64) -> s64 {
    p := get(b, index);
    if p == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return p.*;
}

/// How many buckets are allocated — `count` rounded up to a bucket.
///
/// Exposed because it is the overhead a caller pays for stability, and a caller choosing between this and
/// `List` should be able to measure it rather than reason about it.
bucket_count :: (b: *Bucket_Array) -> s64 {
    if b.count == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return (b.count - 1) / BUCKET_SIZE + 1;
}

/// Frees every bucket and the spine.
///
/// The array is empty afterwards and may be pushed to again, which is what makes this safe to call in a
/// `defer` beside the `make`.
free_all :: (b: *Bucket_Array) {
    spine := view(b.spine.data, b.spine.count);
    i := 0;
    while i < b.spine.count {
        free(untyped(spine[i].slots));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if b.spine.data != null {
        free(untyped(b.spine.data));
    }
    b.spine.data = null;
    b.spine.count = 0;
    b.spine.capacity = 0;
    b.count = 0;
}

/// Appends one bucket pointer to the spine, doubling its storage when full.
///
/// The spine's own growth **copies**, which is exactly what the buckets must not do — and is harmless,
/// because nothing outside this module holds a pointer to the spine. That asymmetry is the design: one
/// level that may move, one that may not.
///
/// Doubling from four, matching `List` (ADR-0107): a fixed increment makes a sequence of appends `O(n²)`.
/// `false` when the spine cannot grow, which the caller turns into a `null` push.
push_bucket :: (spine: *[..]Bucket, bucket: Bucket) -> bool {
    if spine.count == spine.capacity {
        grown := 4;
        if spine.capacity > 0 {
            grown = spine.capacity * 2;
        }
        fresh := typed(Bucket, malloc(grown * size_of(Bucket)));
        if fresh == null {
            return false;
        }
        old := view(spine.data, spine.count);
        new := view(fresh, spine.count);
        i := 0;
        while i < spine.count {
            new[i] = old[i];
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if spine.data != null {
            free(untyped(spine.data));
        }
        spine.data = fresh;
        spine.capacity = grown;
    }
    slots := view(spine.data, spine.capacity);
    slots[spine.count] = bucket;
    spine.count = spine.count + 1;
    return true;
}
